{"name":"Port Wine Barrel","hex":"#846342","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"port","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A place on the coast at which ships can shelter, or dock to load and unload cargo or passengers.","A town or city containing such a place, a port city."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to port, the left-hand side of a vessel when facing the bow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/port"},{"term":"wine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An alcoholic beverage made by fermenting grape juice, with an ABV ranging from 5.5–16%.","An alcoholic beverage made by fermenting other substances, producing a similar ABV."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To entertain (someone) with wine.","To drink wine."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wine"},{"term":"barrel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A round (cylindrical) vessel, such as a cask, of greater length than breadth, and bulging in the middle, made of staves bound with hoops, and having flat ends (heads). The word is sometimes applied to a similar cylindrical container made of metal, usually called a drum.","Such a cask of a certain size, holding one-eighth of what a tun holds. (See a diagram comparing cask sizes.)"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To put or to pack in a barrel or barrels.","To move quickly or in an uncontrolled manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/barrel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Oak (wine)","blocks":[{"text":"Oak is used in winemaking to vary the color, flavor, tannin profile and texture of wine. It can be introduced in the form of a barrel during the fermentation or aging periods, or as free-floating chips or staves added to wine fermented in a vessel like stainless steel. Oak barrels can impart other qualities to wine through evaporation and low level exposure to oxygen."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Wine_Barrels.jpg/330px-Wine_Barrels.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oak_(wine)"},{"title":"Port wine","blocks":[{"text":"Port wine, or simply port, is a Portuguese fortified wine produced in the Douro Valley of northern Portugal. It is typically a sweet red wine, often served with dessert, although it also comes in dry, semi-dry, and white varieties."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Port_wine.jpg/330px-Port_wine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_wine"},{"title":"Port","blocks":[{"text":"A port is a maritime facility comprising one or more wharves or loading areas, where ships load and discharge cargo and passengers. Although usually situated on a sea coast or estuary, ports can also be found far inland, such as Hamburg, Manchester and Duluth; these access the sea via rivers or canals. Because of their roles as ports of entry for immigrants as well as soldiers in wartime, many port cities have experienced dramatic multi-ethnic and multicultural changes throughout their histories."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Manhattan.jpg/330px-Manhattan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port"},{"title":"Wine","blocks":[{"text":"Wine is an alcoholic drink made from fermented grape juice. It is produced and consumed in many regions around the world, in a wide variety of styles which are influenced by different varieties of grapes, growing environments, viticulture methods, and production techniques."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Red_and_white_wine_in_glass.jpg/330px-Red_and_white_wine_in_glass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wine"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipe